From f935b47f72b6f2c865bf6f265c03c1f0b8f718db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:43:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Let a sideways drag along the bar change brightness or volume

Two things people reach for constantly while reading, and both of them are
somewhere else: brightness behind a shade pulled down from the top of the
screen, volume on the side of the phone. The bar is already under the thumb.

Off by default, and off is the bar exactly as it was. Nothing intercepts, no
preference is written, and a profile that has never opened the setting behaves
the way every earlier build did.

Nothing is claimed on the way down. A button needs the press to know it has
been pressed and needs to keep it while the finger is still, so the bar watches
and does not take. Only once the finger has travelled past Android's own touch
slop, and travelled further sideways than up or down, does the bar take the
gesture; the framework then tells the button its touch was cancelled, which is
what stops it firing when the finger lifts. Tapping is exactly as forgiving as
it was, holding still is left alone for the long press that comes later, and an
upward drag is left to the system, since the bar sits where the gesture that
puts the phone away begins.

While dragging, the bar becomes the slider rather than growing one. The finger
is on the bar, so the thing that responds should be the bar; something floating
above it would be a second surface to explain and would cover the buttons it is
replacing anyway. The five positions are hidden and given back with exactly the
visibility they had, because one of them may have been hidden for its own
reasons and turning it back on would be answering a question this gesture was
not asked.

Brightness means this window and nothing else. Android hands out the device's
brightness only behind a permission the user grants on a separate screen, which
is a great deal to ask for a gesture on a toolbar, and it would leave the phone
dimmed after Bare was closed. A window that has not set its own brightness
reports a sentinel rather than a number, so the first drag reads what the
device is actually showing and starts there instead of jumping.

Volume is the media stream in the steps the device actually has, without the
system's own volume panel: the bar being dragged is the feedback, and a second
slider appearing over it on every movement would be the louder of the two.

Migration note (156.0.8078.25): upstream gave BottomBarView an onTouchEvent that
always consumes, so touches in the bar's padding never reach the page
(crbug.com/558675943). The swipe handling lives in that one method now and keeps
consuming every touch, rather than returning false when no swipe is set.
